(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze Precision Rectifiers \(Half-Wave and Full-Wave Absolute Value Circuits\), explaining how diode forward drop is eliminated.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Precision Rectifiers \(Super Diodes\):) Tj
T*
(   1. Limitation of Conventional Diodes:) Tj
T*
(   - Silicon diodes require a threshold forward voltage \(~0.7 V\) to conduct. Signals below 0.7 V) Tj
T*
(   cannot be rectified and are completely lost.) Tj
T*
(   2. Precision Half-Wave Rectifier:) Tj
T*
(   - Op-amp with input resistor R, feedback diode D1 directed toward output, and feedback resistor) Tj
T*
(   R in series with diode D2.) Tj
T*
(   - When Vin > 0: Op-amp output swings negative, forward-biasing diode D1 \(clamping op-amp output) Tj
T*
(   to -0.7 V\) while reverse-biasing D2. Output Vout = 0 V.) Tj
T*
(   - When Vin < 0: Op-amp output swings positive. Diode D2 is forward biased, closing the negative) Tj
T*
(   feedback loop through resistor R.) Tj
T*
(   - The virtual ground constraint forces: Vout = - \(R / R\) * Vin = - Vin \(Positive output\).) Tj
T*
(   - The diode forward drop VD is divided by the open-loop gain of the op-amp:) Tj
T*
(   V_drop_effective = VD / Aol approx 0.7 V / 10^5 = 7 microvolts!) Tj
T*
(   - Successfully rectifies microvolt-level signals without distortion.) Tj
T*
ET

