(>> Solution to Question 12:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Active Filter Approximation Functions: Compare Butterworth, Chebyshev, Bessel, and Elliptic filters.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Active Filter Approximation Theories:) Tj
T*
(   1. Butterworth Approximation \(Maximally Flat\):) Tj
T*
(   - Magnitude: |H\(j*w\)|^2 = 1 / [ 1 + \(w / wc\)^\(2*n\) ].) Tj
T*
(   - Characteristics: Passband has zero ripple \(maximally flat at w = 0\); smooth monotonic) Tj
T*
(   roll-off \(-20n dB/decade\).) Tj
T*
(   - Poles lie on a circle of radius wc in complex s-plane. Moderate phase linearity.) Tj
T*
(   2. Chebyshev Approximation \(Equiripple\):) Tj
T*
(   - Type I: Passband ripple with monotonic stopband: |H\(j*w\)|^2 = 1 / [ 1 + epsilon^2 * Cn^2\(w /) Tj
T*
(   wc\) ], where Cn is Chebyshev polynomial.) Tj
T*
(   - Type II \(Inverse Chebyshev\): Flat passband with equiripple stopband.) Tj
T*
(   - Characteristics: Significantly steeper transition band than Butterworth for identical filter) Tj
T*
(   order n. Poor phase linearity \(ringing on step inputs\). Poles lie on an ellipse.) Tj
T*
(   3. Bessel \(Thomson\) Approximation \(Maximally Flat Group Delay\):) Tj
T*
(   - Optimized for linear phase response across the passband: d\(phi\) / dw = Constant \(constant) Tj
T*
(   group delay\).) Tj
T*
(   - Characteristics: Zero overshoot and zero ringing in time domain; preserves square pulses) Tj
T*
(   without dispersion. Sluggish roll-off in frequency domain.) Tj
T*
(   4. Elliptic \(Cauer\) Approximation:) Tj
T*
(   - Equiripple in BOTH passband and stopband using Jacobian elliptic functions.) Tj
T*
(   - Characteristics: The steepest possible transition roll-off for a given order n. Severe phase) Tj
T*
ET

