(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Switched-Capacitor Filters \(SCF\), deriving the equivalent resistance of a clocked capacitor.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Switched-Capacitor Filter Theory:) Tj
T*
(   1. Motivation in Monolithic Integrated Circuits:) Tj
T*
(   - Fabricating precise, large-value resistors in standard CMOS technology is problematic:) Tj
T*
(   resistors consume huge silicon area and have poor absolute tolerances \(+-20-30%\).) Tj
T*
(   - In contrast, CMOS capacitors and switch ratios can be fabricated with exquisite relative) Tj
T*
(   accuracy \(< 0.1%\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Equivalent Resistance Derivation:) Tj
T*
(   - A capacitor C is switched between input node V1 and output node V2 by two non-overlapping) Tj
T*
(   complementary MOS clock switches \(phi1 and phi2\) operating at switching frequency f_clk \(period) Tj
T*
(   T = 1/f_clk\).) Tj
T*
(   - During clock phase phi1: Switch 1 closes. Capacitor charges to voltage V1; charge stored is) Tj
T*
(   Q1 = C * V1.) Tj
T*
(   - During clock phase phi2: Switch 1 opens, Switch 2 closes. Capacitor voltage resets to V2;) Tj
T*
(   charge stored is Q2 = C * V2.) Tj
T*
(   - Net charge transferred from node 1 to node 2 during each clock period:) Tj
T*
(   Delta-Q = Q1 - Q2 = C * \(V1 - V2\).) Tj
T*
(   - Average current flowing between nodes:) Tj
T*
(   I_avg = Delta-Q / T = Delta-Q * f_clk = f_clk * C * \(V1 - V2\).) Tj
T*
(   - By Ohm's law, effective equivalent resistance:) Tj
T*
(   R_eq = \(V1 - V2\) / I_avg = 1 / \(f_clk * C\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Salient Advantage:) Tj
T*
(   - Active filter cutoff frequency fc = 1 / \(2*pi * R_eq * C_filter\) = f_clk * [ C / \(2 * pi *) Tj
T*
(   C_filter\) ].) Tj
T*
(   - Filter cutoff is tuned dynamically simply by adjusting external clock frequency f_clk!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 14:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Logarithmic and Anti-Logarithmic Op-Amp Amplifiers, detailing temperature compensation techniques.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Logarithmic and Anti-Logarithmic Amplifiers:) Tj
T*
(   1. Basic Logarithmic Amplifier:) Tj
T*
(   - Input resistor R connected to inverting terminal; BJT transistor Q1 placed in feedback path) Tj
T*
(   \(collector to inverting node, emitter to Vout, base grounded\).) Tj
T*
(   - BJT collector current: Ic = Is * exp\(Vbe / Vt\) => Vbe = Vt * ln\(Ic / Is\).) Tj
T*
(   - By virtual ground \(V- = 0\): Ic = Vin / R.) Tj
T*
(   - Since Vbe = 0 - Vout = - Vout:) Tj
T*
(   Vout = - Vt * ln[ Vin / \(R * Is\) ].) Tj
T*
(   2. Temperature Compensation Challenge:) Tj
T*
(   - Both thermal voltage Vt = kT/q \(~+3300 ppm/deg C\) and reverse saturation current Is \(~doubles) Tj
T*
(   every 10 deg C\) are heavily temperature dependent.) Tj
T*
(   3. Temperature Compensation Architecture:) Tj
T*
(   - Differential Transistor Pair: Uses two matched BJTs \(Q1 and Q2\) on the identical monolithic) Tj
T*
(   substrate.) Tj
T*
(   - Q1 processes input signal Vin; Q2 is biased with a fixed stable reference current Iref:) Tj
T*
(   Vout1 - Vout2 = - Vt * ln\(Vin / R*Is\) - [ - Vt * ln\(Iref / Is\) ] = - Vt * ln[ Vin / \(R * Iref\)) Tj
T*
(   ].) Tj
T*
ET

