(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Analog Multipliers and Dividers constructed using Log, Anti-Log, and Summing operational amplifiers.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Analog Multipliers and Dividers via Log-Antilog Processing:) Tj
T*
(   1. Operational Principle:) Tj
T*
(   - Based on fundamental logarithmic identities:) Tj
T*
(   * Multiplication: ln\(X * Y\) = ln\(X\) + ln\(Y\) => X * Y = exp[ ln\(X\) + ln\(Y\) ].) Tj
T*
(   * Division: ln\(X / Y\) = ln\(X\) - ln\(Y\) => X / Y = exp[ ln\(X\) - ln\(Y\) ].) Tj
T*
(   * Powers / Roots: ln\(X^m\) = m * ln\(X\) => X^m = exp[ m * ln\(X\) ].) Tj
T*
(   2. Analog Multiplier Architecture:) Tj
T*
(   - Inputs Vx and Vy are applied to two separate matched Logarithmic Amplifiers, generating) Tj
T*
(   outputs proportional to ln\(Vx\) and ln\(Vy\).) Tj
T*
(   - The two signals are summed in an Op-Amp Summing Amplifier: V_sum = - k * [ ln\(Vx\) + ln\(Vy\) ]) Tj
T*
(   = - k * ln\(Vx * Vy\).) Tj
T*
(   - The summed output is passed into an Anti-Logarithmic \(Exponential\) Amplifier:) Tj
T*
(   Vout = exp[ - V_sum / k ] = exp[ ln\(Vx * Vy\) ] = \(Vx * Vy\) / Vref.) Tj
T*
(   3. Analog Divider Architecture:) Tj
T*
(   - Inputs Vx and Vy are processed by Log amplifiers.) Tj
T*
(   - A Difference Amplifier subtracts the logarithmic outputs: V_diff = k * [ ln\(Vx\) - ln\(Vy\) ] =) Tj
T*
(   k * ln\(Vx / Vy\).) Tj
T*
(   - Anti-Log stage evaluates exponential: Vout = Vref * \(Vx / Vy\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
