BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze Op-Amp Frequency Compensation: Explain Dominant Pole Compensation, pole-splitting, and phase margin.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Op-Amp Frequency Compensation and Stability:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Problem of Multi-Pole Instability:) Tj
T*
(   - A multi-stage op-amp possesses multiple poles in its open-loop response: A\(s\) = A0 / [ \(1 +) Tj
T*
(   s/p1\)\(1 + s/p2\)\(1 + s/p3\) ].) Tj
T*
(   - Each pole contributes up to 90 degrees of phase lag. At high frequencies, total phase lag) Tj
T*
(   reaches 270 degrees.) Tj
T*
(   - If negative feedback is applied, total phase shift around loop can reach 180 + 180 = 360) Tj
T*
(   degrees \(positive feedback\) while loop gain |A*beta| >= 1, causing violent parasitic) Tj
T*
(   oscillations.) Tj
T*
(   2. Dominant Pole Compensation via Miller Pole-Splitting:) Tj
T*
(   - A tiny compensation capacitor Cc \(~30 pF\) is connected across the high-gain intermediate) Tj
T*
(   inverting stage.) Tj
T*
(   - Miller effect multiplies Cc by stage gain Av2, creating a massive effective capacitance) Tj
T*
(   C_in_Miller = Cc * \(1 + Av2\) at the input of stage 2.) Tj
T*
(   - This pushes the first pole p1 down to a very low dominant frequency \(~5 to 10 Hz!\).) Tj
T*
(   - Simultaneously, negative feedback through Cc pushes the second pole p2 outward to much higher) Tj
T*
(   frequencies \('Pole-Splitting'\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Phase Margin \(PM\):) Tj
T*
(   - PM = 180 deg + Phase[ A\(j*w\) * beta ] at frequency where |A * beta| = 1.0 \(0 dB\).) Tj
T*
ET

