(>> Solution to Question 20:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Bruton's FDNR \(Frequency-Dependent Negative Resistor\) Transformation for realizing active ladder filters without inductors.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Bruton's Transformation and Active Ladder Synthesis:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Inductor Dilemma:) Tj
T*
(   - Doubly terminated passive LC ladder filters have the lowest component sensitivities known.) Tj
T*
(   However, physical magnetic inductors are bulky, lossy, and radiate EMI.) Tj
T*
(   2. Bruton's 1/s Impedance Scaling:) Tj
T*
(   - Divide every impedance in a passive LC prototype by complex frequency s:) Tj
T*
(   Z'\(s\) = Z\(s\) / s.) Tj
T*
(   - Resistor R becomes: Z_R'\(s\) = R / s \(Behaves as a Capacitor of value C' = 1/R\).) Tj
T*
(   - Inductor L becomes: Z_L'\(s\) = \(s * L\) / s = L \(Behaves as a Resistor of value R' = L\).) Tj
T*
(   - Capacitor C becomes: Z_C'\(s\) = [ 1 / \(s * C\) ] / s = 1 / \(s^2 * C\) = 1 / \(s^2 * D\).) Tj
T*
(   3. The D-Element \(FDNR\):) Tj
T*
(   - Defined by Z_D\(s\) = 1 / \(s^2 * D\).) Tj
T*
(   - Along the j*omega axis: Z_D\(j*w\) = 1 / [ \(j*w\)^2 * D ] = - 1 / \(w^2 * D\).) Tj
T*
(   - A real, negative, frequency-dependent resistance!) Tj
T*
(   - Realized electronically using Antoniou's General Impedance Converter \(GIC\) with two op-amps,) Tj
T*
(   two capacitors, and three resistors.) Tj
T*
(   - Result: Replaces floating inductors with grounded FDNRs, preserving the legendary low) Tj
T*
(   sensitivity of LC ladder filters.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
