(   - Conventional CMOS op-amps suffer from substantial 1/f \(flicker\) noise and DC input offset) Tj
T*
(   voltage drift \(~microvolts/deg C\) due to random transistor mismatch.) Tj
T*
(   2. Chopping Principle:) Tj
T*
(   - Modulation: The input DC/low-frequency signal is chopped \(multiplied by a square wave carrier) Tj
T*
(   at clock frequency f_ch ~100 kHz\), modulating the signal up to high frequency f_ch.) Tj
T*
(   - Amplification: The modulated signal is amplified by the op-amp stage. Crucially, the) Tj
T*
(   amplifier's internal DC offset and 1/f noise remain unmodulated at DC.) Tj
T*
(   - Demodulation: A synchronous second chopper demodulates the amplified signal back down to) Tj
T*
(   baseband \(DC\).) Tj
T*
(   - Offset and Noise Shift: The internal offset and 1/f noise pass through the second chopper and) Tj
T*
(   are modulated UP to f_ch.) Tj
T*
(   - Low-Pass Filtering: A post-filter strips away the chopped high-frequency noise spikes at) Tj
T*
(   f_ch.) Tj
T*
(   3. Performance Outcome:) Tj
T*
(   - Achieves near-zero offset voltage \(< 1 microvolt\) and virtually zero temperature drift \(<) Tj
T*
(   0.005 uV/deg C\) with complete elimination of 1/f flicker noise.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
