12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Phase Margin and Gain Margin in feedback amplifiers using Bode Plots.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Gain Margin and Phase Margin in Feedback Amplifiers:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definitions from Loop Gain T\(s\) = A\(s\) * beta:) Tj
T*
(   - Gain Crossover Frequency \(w_gc\): Frequency where loop gain magnitude is unity \(0 dB\):) Tj
T*
(   |T\(j*w_gc\)| = 1.0 \(0 dB\).) Tj
T*
(   - Phase Crossover Frequency \(w_pc\): Frequency where loop phase lag reaches -180 degrees:) Tj
T*
(   Angle[T\(j*w_pc\)] = -180 deg.) Tj
T*
(   2. Phase Margin \(PM\):) Tj
T*
(   - The additional phase lag required at the gain crossover frequency to induce instability:) Tj
T*
(   PM = 180 deg + Angle[ T\(j*w_gc\) ].) Tj
T*
(   - If Angle is -120 deg: PM = 180 - 120 = +60 degrees \(highly stable, well-damped transient) Tj
T*
(   response\).) Tj
T*
(   - If PM <= 0, circuit is unstable and oscillates.) Tj
T*
(   3. Gain Margin \(GM\):) Tj
T*
(   - The factor by which loop gain can be increased before instability occurs at phase crossover:) Tj
T*
(   GM \(dB\) = - 20 * log10 | T\(j*w_pc\) |.) Tj
T*
(   - Positive GM \(> 10-12 dB\) ensures robust gain tolerance.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
