(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the Op-Amp Voltage-to-Current Converter \(Transconductance Amplifier\) for Floating and Grounded Loads.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Voltage-to-Current Converters \(Transconductance Amplifiers\):) Tj
T*
(   1. Floating Load V-to-I Converter:) Tj
T*
(   - Inverting configuration with load impedance ZL placed directly in the feedback path, and) Tj
T*
(   input Vin connected through R1.) Tj
T*
(   - By virtual ground \(V- = 0\): Current through R1 is I_in = Vin / R1.) Tj
T*
(   - Since I- = 0, the identical current flows through load ZL:) Tj
T*
(   I_L = Vin / R1.) Tj
T*
(   - Load current is strictly independent of load impedance ZL and load voltage drop!) Tj
T*
(   2. Howland Current Pump \(Grounded Load V-to-I Converter\):) Tj
T*
(   - Circuit utilizes both negative and positive feedback to drive a load ZL connected to ground.) Tj
T*
(   - Resistors R1, R2, R3, R4 configured in bridge with op-amp.) Tj
T*
(   - When balanced \(R2 / R1 = R4 / R3\):) Tj
T*
(   Output load current:) Tj
T*
(   I_L = Vin / R1.) Tj
T*
(   - Demonstrates infinite output impedance; load current remains constant regardless of whether) Tj
T*
(   load is shorted, resistive, or inductive.) Tj
T*
