(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the Phase-Locked Loop \(PLL\), detailing Phase Detector, Loop Filter, and Voltage-Controlled Oscillator \(VCO\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Phase-Locked Loop \(PLL\) Architecture and Operation:) Tj
T*
(   1. Operational Concept:) Tj
T*
(   - A closed-loop feedback control system that synchronizes its output signal in frequency and) Tj
T*
(   phase with an incoming reference signal.) Tj
T*
(   2. Three Functional Building Blocks:) Tj
T*
(   \(a\) Phase Detector \(PD\): Compares phase of input reference fin with phase of feedback signal) Tj
T*
(   f_out. Produces error voltage V_error proportional to phase difference Delta-phi. \(Implemented) Tj
T*
(   via XOR gate, analog multiplier, or phase-frequency detector PFD\).) Tj
T*
(   \(b\) Loop Filter \(Low-Pass Filter\): Strips away high-frequency sum and switching ripple) Tj
T*
(   components from phase detector output, generating clean DC control voltage V_ctrl. Governs PLL) Tj
T*
(   loop dynamics, capture range, and lock time.) Tj
T*
(   \(c\) Voltage-Controlled Oscillator \(VCO\): Free-running oscillator whose output frequency is) Tj
T*
(   linearly modulated by control voltage: f_out = f0 + Kv * V_ctrl.) Tj
T*
(   3. Operating States:) Tj
T*
(   - Free-Running State: No input signal present; VCO runs at natural center frequency f0.) Tj
T*
(   - Capture State: Input applied; PLL dynamically adjusts control voltage to pull VCO toward fin.) Tj
T*
(   - Phase Lock State: f_out matches fin exactly with constant static phase offset.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 27:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the State-Variable Band-Pass Filter, deriving formulas for center frequency, bandwidth, and peak gain.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   State-Variable Band-Pass Filter Analysis:) Tj
T*
(   1. Transfer Function:) Tj
T*
(   - H_BP\(s\) = - \(H0 * \(omega_0 / Q\) * s\) / [ s^2 + \(omega_0 / Q\) * s + omega_0^2 ].) Tj
T*
(   2. Frequency Response:) Tj
T*
(   - Substitute s = j*omega:) Tj
T*
(   H_BP\(j*w\) = - [ j * H0 * \(w0 / Q\) * w ] / [ \(w0^2 - w^2\) + j * \(w0 / Q\) * w ].) Tj
T*
(   3. Center \(Resonant\) Frequency:) Tj
T*
(   - Occurs when denominator real part vanishes: w0^2 - w^2 = 0 => w = w0 = 1 / \(R * C\).) Tj
T*
(   - Peak Gain at w0:) Tj
T*
(   |H_BP\(j*w0\)| = H0 \(Set by input resistor ratio\).) Tj
T*
(   - Phase shift at center frequency is exactly 180 degrees.) Tj
T*
(   4. Bandwidth and Cutoff Frequencies:) Tj
T*
(   - Half-power frequencies w1 and w2 occur where gain drops to H0 / sqrt\(2\):) Tj
T*
(   w0^2 - w^2 = +- \(w0 / Q\) * w.) Tj
T*
(   - Bandwidth: BW = w2 - w1 = w0 / Q.) Tj
T*
(   - Higher Q narrows bandwidth, creating extreme selective resonance.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
