(   [Problem Statement]: Describe Instrumentation Operational Amplifiers in Biomedical Engineering: Bio-potential acquisition \(ECG/EEG\) and Right-Leg Drive circuits.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Biomedical Bio-Potential Acquisition and Right-Leg Drive:) Tj
T*
(   1. Biophysical Signal Challenges:) Tj
T*
(   - Electrocardiogram \(ECG\) signals have microvolt-to-millivolt amplitudes \(0.5 - 4 mV\) with) Tj
T*
(   frequencies 0.05 - 150 Hz.) Tj
T*
(   - Accompanied by massive Common-Mode 50/60 Hz power-line interference induced by capacitive) Tj
T*
(   coupling with room wiring \(can exceed 1-2 Volts!\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Front-End Instrumentation Amplifier:) Tj
T*
(   - High input impedance \(> 10^10 Ohms\) matches high skin-electrode contact impedance.) Tj
T*
(   - Ultra-high CMRR \(> 110 dB\) suppresses common-mode noise while amplifying differential cardiac) Tj
T*
(   action potentials.) Tj
T*
(   3. Driven Right-Leg \(DRL\) Circuit:) Tj
T*
(   - Rather than grounding patient's right leg directly, common-mode voltage Vcm is picked off) Tj
T*
(   from input resistors, inverted and amplified by an auxiliary op-amp, and injected back into the) Tj
T*
(   patient's right leg.) Tj
T*
